**Setup.** Let the $m$ distinct positive even numbers be $2a_1<\cdots<2a_m$ (distinct positive integers $a_i$), and the $n$ distinct positive odd numbers be $2b_1-1<\cdots<2b_n-1$ (distinct positive integers $b_i$). The even sum is $2\sum a_i \ge 2(1+\cdots+m)=m(m+1)$, and the odd sum is $2\sum b_i - n \ge 2(1+\cdots+n)-n = n^2$. Since $1987$ is odd and the even sum is even, $n$ must be odd. The achievable even sums are exactly all even integers $\ge m(m+1)$, and the achievable odd sums (with $n$ odd) are exactly all odd integers $\ge n^2$. So the real constraint is
$$m(m+1)+n^2 \le 1987,\qquad n\ge 1\text{ odd},\ m\ge 0.$$

**Upper bound via Cauchy–Schwarz.** Set $u=m+\tfrac12$, so $m(m+1)=u^2-\tfrac14$. The constraint gives $u^2+n^2\le 1987+\tfrac14=1987.25$. Then
$$3m+4n = 3u+4n-\tfrac32.$$
